3. Historical data and source checks
The Microsoft FY2026 Form 10-K was used to check operating cash flow of $182,935 million, capital expenditures of $115,948 million, interest expense of $3,051 million, and tax inputs used in the after-tax interest calculation. The revised starting FCFF is:
$182,935m + $2,459.2199m − $115,948m = $69,446.2199m.
The earlier starting FCFF was $69,408.2199m. Correcting the operating cash flow and capital expenditure inputs increased the FCFF valuation from approximately $197.60/share to $197.71/share. The Microsoft closing price of $501.02 on September 1, 2026 was checked against Yahoo Finance historical data.
Outstanding check: The Oracle and Adobe historical share prices and EPS measurement conventions in the P/E model have not been independently verified for consistent valuation-date treatment. Their peer-implied result remains a provisional cross-check.
4. Accounting and numerical validation
The base-case pro-forma model passed its reported balance-sheet consistency checks. The FCFF, FCFE, peer-comparison, and reconciliation outputs were reproduced using the project Python scripts. These checks support internal reproducibility; they do not establish that the FCFF and FCFE models are economically equivalent.
The FCFF DCF uses a 10% WACC, 3% terminal growth rate, and five-year FCFF growth path of 15%, 13%, 11%, 9%, and 7%. Approximately 74.28% of modeled enterprise value comes from terminal value, making the estimate sensitive to long-run assumptions.
